# Terms added in the 2026 expansion
GLOSARIO += [
    ('Ancient DNA', 'DNA recovered from remains hundreds or thousands of years old, such as bones or teeth. It is usually highly fragmented and contaminated. <a href="../human-evolution/atapuerca-and-ancient-dna/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Cell-free DNA', 'Fragments of DNA floating freely in the blood. Some may come from a tumour or, in pregnancy, from the placenta. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/liquid-biopsy/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Recombinant DNA', 'DNA made in the laboratory by joining fragments from different sources, often from different species. <a href="../people/paul-berg/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Aneuploidy', 'An abnormal number of chromosomes in a cell, too many or too few, as in trisomy 21 or Turner syndrome. <a href="../diseases/turner-syndrome/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Monoclonal antibody', 'An immune-system protein made in the laboratory to recognise a specific target, such as a tumour protein. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/precision-oncology/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Apoptosis', 'Programmed cell death: an orderly process by which the body removes damaged or unneeded cells. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/how-a-tumour-begins/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Pedigree', 'A drawing using standard symbols that shows a family\'s members and who has a condition. It helps work out how it is inherited. <a href="../heredity/inheritance-patterns/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Bacteriophage', 'A virus that infects bacteria. Studying phages gave key clues about DNA and CRISPR.'),
    ('Biobank', 'An organised collection of biological samples and data from many people for research, with their consent. <a href="../genetics-and-society/genetic-data-privacy/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Liquid biopsy', 'A blood test that looks for tumour DNA to diagnose or monitor cancer without surgery. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/liquid-biopsy/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Cas9', 'A bacterial protein that cuts DNA at the point specified by a guide RNA. It is the most widely used CRISPR tool. <a href="../treatments/crispr-explained/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Germ cell', 'A cell that gives rise to eggs or sperm. Changes in it can be passed to children.'),
    ('Stem cell', 'A cell able to divide and to turn into other cell types.'),
    ('Centromere', 'The narrow region of a chromosome that joins its two arms and by which the cell pulls it during division. <a href="../basics/genes-chromosomes-and-genome/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Clone', 'An organism or cell with the same DNA as the one it came from. <a href="../plants-and-animals/animal-cloning/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Codominance', 'When both variants of a gene are expressed together, as in the AB blood group. <a href="../everyday-traits/blood-groups/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Collagen', 'The body\'s most abundant protein, which gives strength to skin, bone, tendons and vessels. <a href="../diseases/ehlers-danlos-syndromes/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Chromatid', 'Each of the two identical copies of a chromosome after it has been duplicated, joined at the centromere.'),
    ('Chromatin', 'The mix of DNA and proteins that makes up chromosomes. It can be more or less tightly packed. <a href="../heredity/epigenetics/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Deletion', 'Loss of a piece of DNA, from a single letter to part of a chromosome. <a href="../basics/mutations-and-variants/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Denisovans', 'An extinct human group known mainly from its DNA, which left traces in the genomes of populations in Asia and Oceania. <a href="../human-evolution/neanderthals-and-denisovans/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Genetic drift', 'Random change in the frequency of genetic variants from one generation to the next, stronger in small populations. <a href="../human-evolution/out-of-africa/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Prenatal diagnosis', 'Tests to find out whether a fetus has a genetic or chromosomal condition. <a href="../pregnancy-and-reproduction/prenatal-diagnosis-step-by-step/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Uniparental disomy', 'When both copies of a chromosome come from the same parent. <a href="../diseases/prader-willi-and-angelman-syndromes/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Double helix', 'The shape of DNA: two strands wound around each other, joined by their bases. <a href="../basics/what-is-dna/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Duplication', 'An extra copy of a piece of DNA. <a href="../basics/mutations-and-variants/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Founder effect', 'A high frequency of a variant in a population descended from a few ancestors who carried it. <a href="../rare-diseases/what-is-a-rare-disease/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Electrophoresis', 'A technique that separates DNA or protein fragments by size by moving them through a gel with an electric current. <a href="../forensic-genetics/dna-fingerprinting/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Rare disease', 'In Europe, one affecting fewer than 5 in 10,000 people. About three in four have a genetic cause. <a href="../rare-diseases/what-is-a-rare-disease/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Enzyme', 'A protein that speeds up a chemical reaction in the body. <a href="../basics/from-gene-to-protein/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Restriction enzyme', 'A bacterial enzyme that cuts DNA at a specific sequence. It was key to genetic engineering.'),
    ('Specificity', 'A test\'s ability to give a negative result in people who do not have the condition. <a href="../diagnosis/how-to-read-a-genetic-result/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Variable expressivity', 'Differences in severity or symptoms of the same genetic condition among people with the same variant. <a href="../diseases/neurofibromatosis-type-1/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('In vitro fertilisation', 'Joining egg and sperm in the laboratory, then transferring the embryo to the womb. <a href="../pregnancy-and-reproduction/ivf-with-preimplantation-testing/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Gamete', 'A reproductive cell, egg or sperm. It has half the chromosomes of other cells. <a href="../pregnancy-and-reproduction/egg-and-sperm-donation/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Identical twins', 'Twins from a single embryo that split; they share almost all their DNA. <a href="../faq/do-twins-have-the-same-dna/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Tumour suppressor gene', 'A gene that restrains cell division or repairs damage. Losing both copies promotes cancer. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/how-a-tumour-begins/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Genetic genealogy', 'Using consumer DNA databases to find distant relatives, including to identify suspects. <a href="../forensic-genetics/genetic-genealogy/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Haplogroup', 'A group of people sharing a common ancestor through the maternal (mitochondrial DNA) or paternal (Y chromosome) line. <a href="../human-evolution/out-of-africa/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Haplotype', 'A set of nearby variants on a chromosome that tend to be inherited together.'),
    ('Hemizygous', 'Having only one copy of a gene, as men do for X-chromosome genes. <a href="../heredity/inheritance-patterns/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('DNA fingerprint', 'A profile of DNA markers that distinguishes one person from almost everyone else. <a href="../forensic-genetics/dna-fingerprinting/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Gene drive', 'A genetic construct inherited by almost all offspring that can spread through a population. <a href="../plants-and-animals/modified-mosquitoes-against-malaria/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('X inactivation', 'The random switching-off of one of the two X chromosomes in each cell of females. <a href="../people/mary-lyon/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Insertion', 'The addition of one or more extra letters to a DNA sequence. <a href="../basics/mutations-and-variants/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Inversion', 'A piece of a chromosome that has been flipped round.'),
    ('Lysosome', 'A cell organelle full of enzymes that break down and recycle molecules. <a href="../diseases/tay-sachs-and-lysosomal-diseases/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Genetic marker', 'A DNA variant at a known position used as a signpost to follow inheritance or identify people.'),
    ('Mendelian', 'A trait or condition that depends on a single gene and follows Mendel\'s laws. <a href="../heredity/mendels-laws/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Metastasis', 'The spread of a cancer from where it started to other organs. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/how-a-tumour-begins/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Mitochondrion', "The organelle that produces the cell's energy. It has its own DNA, inherited from the mother."),
    ('Mitosis', 'Division of a cell into two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes.'),
    ('Neanderthals', 'A human species that lived in Europe and Asia until about 40,000 years ago and interbred with our ancestors. <a href="../human-evolution/neanderthals-and-denisovans/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Antisense oligonucleotide', 'A short synthetic piece of RNA or DNA that binds a specific RNA to change how it is used or destroy it. <a href="../diseases/hereditary-als/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Oncogene', 'A gene that, when altered, speeds up cell division and promotes cancer. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/how-a-tumour-begins/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Genetically modified organism', 'A living thing whose DNA has been changed using genetic engineering. <a href="../plants-and-animals/gm-and-gene-edited-crops/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Palaeogenomics', 'The study of whole genomes of ancient or extinct organisms. <a href="../people/svante-paabo/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Pleiotropy', 'The effect of a single gene on several different traits or organs. <a href="../diseases/marfan-syndrome/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Polygenic', 'A trait that depends on many genes, each with a small effect, such as height. <a href="../everyday-traits/height/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Polymorphism', 'A genetic variant common in the population, usually with no harmful effect.'),
    ('Protein', 'A molecule made of a chain of amino acids that carries out almost all of the cell\'s work. <a href="../basics/from-gene-to-protein/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Chimera', 'An organism made of cells from two genetically different individuals.'),
    ('Recombination', 'The exchange of segments between matching chromosomes during meiosis, which shuffles parental genes. <a href="../faq/why-are-siblings-different/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Replication', 'Copying of DNA before a cell divides. <a href="../basics/what-is-dna/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Messenger RNA', 'An RNA copy of a gene that carries instructions to the ribosomes to make a protein. <a href="../people/katalin-kariko/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Next-generation sequencing', 'Methods that read millions of DNA fragments at once, allowing whole genomes to be studied quickly and cheaply. <a href="../diagnosis/panels-exome-and-genome/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Natural selection', 'The process by which variants that favour survival and reproduction become more common. <a href="../everyday-traits/lactose-intolerance/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Sensitivity', 'A test\'s ability to give a positive result in people who do have the condition. <a href="../diagnosis/how-to-read-a-genetic-result/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('STR', 'Short tandem repeats: sequences of a few letters repeated a number of times that varies between people. The basis of forensic profiles. <a href="../forensic-genetics/dna-fingerprinting/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Syndrome', 'A set of signs and symptoms that occur together and characterise a condition.'),
    ('Telomere', 'The end of a chromosome, which protects it and shortens with each cell division.'),
    ('Targeted therapy', 'A cancer treatment designed to act on a specific genetic change in the tumour. <a href="../genetics-and-cancer/precision-oncology/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Transgenic', 'An organism that has been given a gene from another species. <a href="../plants-and-animals/gm-and-gene-edited-crops/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Triplet', 'A group of three DNA or RNA letters. A codon is a triplet that corresponds to an amino acid. <a href="../people/har-gobind-khorana/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Adeno-associated virus', 'A harmless virus used as a vector in many gene therapies. <a href="../treatments/gene-therapy/">Read more</a>.'),
    ('Incomplete penetrance', 'When not everyone with a disease-causing variant develops the condition. <a href="../diseases/hereditary-als/">Read more</a>.'),
]

